sub:assertion {
  <https://w3id.org/delphi/resource/approach/printed-stress-aligned-lattice-beats-uniform--grok-4-7--2> a prov:Plan, delphi:TestableHypothesis ;
    rdfs:label "Does assumed strut-radius noise erase the gain?" ;
    dct:subject <https://w3id.org/delphi/resource/problem/printed-stress-aligned-lattice-beats-uniform> ;
    dct:description "On the meshes from the ideal comparison, multiply each strut radius by an independent lognormal draw. Two coefficients of variation, 0.05 and 0.10, and 30 draws each, are assumed stand-ins, not measurements from a printer. They are frozen before the sweep. If a fetched paper states a measured stereolithography radius coefficient for a tough resin, that value replaces 0.05 before any draw is looked at, and the replacement is recorded. The advantage survives at 0.05 only if the 5th percentile of the aligned failure-load proxy still exceeds the 95th percentile of the uniform lattice. The 0.10 runs are reported and are not required to pass." ;

    delphi:proposedTest "At coefficient 0.05, the aligned 5th percentile exceeds the uniform 95th percentile on every load case. The 0.10 ensemble is reported beside it." ;
    delphi:context "Fails if: At coefficient 0.05 the two failure-load distributions overlap by the percentile rule on any load case, so the ideal gain does not survive this noise model." ;
    delphi:hypothesisType "approach/computation" ;
    skos:note "Steps: 1. Freeze the two coefficients and the percentile rule before perturbing radii. 2. Search once for a measured printer coefficient; adopt it only if the page is fetched, and only before the sweep. 3. Run 30 draws at each coefficient on all three load cases. 4. Compare the 5th percentile of the aligned layout with the 95th of the uniform layout. 5. Do not retune the coefficient after seeing the ranking." ;
    skos:note "Difficulty (proposer's estimate of completion within about a month of agent time): tractable" ;
    skos:note "Missing: A measured strut-radius coefficient for the resin and printer that would actually be used. The coefficients 0.05 and 0.10 are assumptions." ;
    skos:note "Tool: fenics" ;
    skos:note "Tool: python" ;
    skos:related <https://w3id.org/delphi/resource/claim/thompson1917-ch16-05> ;
    skos:related <https://w3id.org/delphi/resource/claim/thompson1917-ch16-06> ;

    rdfs:seeAlso <https://doi.org/10.1038/nature23911> ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://github.com/fractastical/delphi/blob/ee98025a1960b300fcc06aa2106f51118b60f565/problems/approaches/grok-4-7.json> .
  <https://w3id.org/delphi/resource/problem/printed-stress-aligned-lattice-beats-uniform> a delphi:Concept, schema:Question ;
    rdfs:label "Does a stress-aligned lattice beat a uniform lattice at equal mass, when printed?" .


  <https://doi.org/10.1038/nature23911> dct:title "Giga-voxel computational morphogenesis for structural design" ;
    dct:bibliographicCitation "Aage, Andreassen, Lazarov and Sigmund 2017, Giga-voxel computational morphogenesis for structural design" .
}

